// Test: native comparison symbols ≠ ≤ ≥
// Spec: 01-LEXICAL.md — native symbol vocabulary
// Priority: P1 — regression guard for defect S10
//
// These three symbols were absent from the lexer. Because the lexer skips
// anything logos does not recognise, they produced NO TOKEN AT ALL rather than
// an error, so `a ≠ 2` silently lexed as `a 2` and evaluated to `a`. Both
// `1 ≠ 2` and `1 ≠ 1` printed the same value.
//
// This pins semantics, not just parsing — a test that only checked "it parses"
// would have passed against the broken lexer.

rite main() {
    ≔ a = 1;

    println(a ≠ 2);   // true
    println(a ≠ 1);   // false
    println(a ≤ 1);   // true
    println(a ≤ 0);   // false
    println(a ≥ 1);   // true
    println(a ≥ 2);   // false

    // and in condition position, where they previously failed to parse
    ⎇ a ≠ 2 { println("ne_cond"); }
    ⎇ a ≤ 1 { println("le_cond"); }
    ⎇ a ≥ 1 { println("ge_cond"); }
}
